[
    {
        "text": "Blocks are made up of messages that say something like I Alice, pay 50,000 coins to Bob, and here is my signature to prove I authorized this transaction. That message gets passed around the peer to peer network as users check that the signature matches Alice's public key. If it doesn't, they'll ignore it and the transaction won't get validated. So we figured out how to stop people from spending Alice's funds. What we haven't figured out is how to prevent Alice from spending the same funds twice. How do we decide which transaction to include in the blockchain? Who decides? How do we reach a consensus decision in a distributed system? We will answer these questions in the next module.",
